A||N||3||Hydroflight||Model Selection for new project||||1||
Z||000000||Hydroflight||06-03-2001||01:52 AM||||Gentlemen/Ladies,<BR>I am in search for a Fiero model that will support my intended objective.  I like the body style and feel the mid engine platform should be able to support my mission.<BR>The mission is to run circles around the various flavor Porsche 911's and pull the Z-06 Vett in a straight line.<BR>Research to date seam's to point to the 1988 GT with a Pressurized Northstar.  My major concern is the handling of the 1988 GT platform.  Does any one have any information that explains the differences in the 88 suspension / platform rigidity versus previous models.  I have checked out the 86 Fiero GT and found it to be inadequate to meet the objective. If the Fiero platform can support the objective I will be in the market for a Black 1988 GT with auto trans and all the options for anyone wishing to part with one.<BR>I sincerely appreciate any help you fine people could afford me.<BR>Thank's in advance.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||rodmcneill||06-03-2001||02:06 AM||||Welcome to the Forum, this message will self destruct in .......||||reg||12
Z||000002||Nashco||06-03-2001||03:52 AM||||Welcome to the forum...you'll soon learn there's a lot of knowledge on this site and it's invaluable 99% of the time.<P>As far as the ridigity of the 88 versus others, as far as I know there's no additional chassis rigidity.  The 88 came with a rear sway bar that attributed to rigidity, but you can add one to other years easily.  The big difference between the early models and the 88 was the actual suspension, everything that attaches to the chassis.  The suspension used in earlier models was an attempt at adapting an already tried and true suspension from one car into another.  I won't even try to explain the technical differences, but basically the geometry was altered to allow several improvements.<P>Just thought of one more thing that would be adding to ridigity of the car; the 88s have no bushings inbetween the engine subframe (cradle), and the car.  This attributes to less flex between the drivetrain and car.<P>I'm too tired to go into more detail <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> so good luck.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Iron Duke Wellingsley||06-03-2001||04:14 AM||||You might be asking too much of a Fiero.  A Porsche 911 is a very powerful car.  <P>The good news is that the suspension can be upgraded:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/suspen.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/suspen.htm</A> <P>There are a number of engine swaps you might consider to get your power.  A blown or <BR>turbo Northstar is one way.  A nitrous SBC V8 could be another.  <P>Archie is the SBC V8 swap guy:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/</A> <P>From what I have read, a Fiero with a Series II 3800 supercharged Buick V6 engine is very fast.  Just search the Forum, there are lots of posts on this swap.  Most people say the Buick V6 3800 II is the ideal engine, with or without a supercharger.  <P>Why is it necessary to beat Porsches and Vettes?  Your mission is to be dissapointed I fear.  The Fiero is an impressive 1980s car.   You could build it to beat most any other 1980s car.  But, the new porsches and vettes are 20 years more advanced.  <P>Can you imagine a 48 chevy out-running a 68 SS model?  What about a 76 mustang cobra II vs. a 96 Mustang cobra.  <P>In each of the above comparisons, radical modifications and big money can't make up for improvements in electronics, design, and materials.  <P>Your Fiero will be unbelievable with 270HP or more.  But, it won't beat a 350 HP Porsche.  Even if you put 400 HP in your Fiero, how can you get that much power to the ground?<P>You need some big rear tires.  Maybe 285s or wider.  But the fatest tires on earth won't make up for electronic traction control.  The best CNC machined racing suspension for a Fiero is no match for active suspension found on today's exotics.  Of cours, they cost hundreds of thousands of dollars.  <P>The good news is that very few people have the more recent high end 911s.   I do believe the remaining cars can be beaten by a high-dollar modified Fiero.  I bet you can get 0 to 60 in 5.5 seconds and a top speed of 150 MPH. <P>If you are serious about those specific goals, my suggetion would be turbocharged 16 valve aluminum block Super Duty 3.0L 4cylinder, probably more than 500 HP if you get one of the old GTP Fiero mechanics to build it for you. <P>The stock aerodynamics may make any speed above 130 MPH terrifying however.  You might consider the Super Duty Fiero body kit.  Here's a place that sells it:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.showcars-bodyparts.com/fiero.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.showcars-bodyparts.com/fiero.html</A> <P>You've got your work cut out for you.  A body kit, engine swap and full suspension upgrade (don't forget the brakes) will take a lot of time and money to complete.  When you get your Fiero together, post it here so we can all drool over it.  I would kill to see pictures of a street legal replica of the Super Duty Fiero racing car with a turbo dohc aluminum super duty engine.  Here's one place that sells the Super Duty:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/prod1.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gtsonline.com/krp/prod1.htm</A> <P>Be careful what you wish for.  Such a car could be so fast that you go insane with speed and are never seen again . . .||||reg||1
